  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nThe ridge will remain in place through a least Saturday, though it \nis expected to start weakening on Friday. Despite the gradually \nfalling heights, persistent deep southwesterly flow will continue to \nadvect warmer temperatures into the region, with mean 850mb \ntemperatures well above 20C on both days. That southwesterly \nflow will also keep the baroclinic zone to our north, limiting \nprecip chances and cloud coverage. The only saving grace for \nFriday and Saturday is that our humidity will be a bit lower, \nparticularly on Saturday. Nevertheless, our heat index reading \nwill top out right around 100 on both days, especially for areas\nalong and south of I-70. With that, we've decided to extend the\nHeat Advisory through Saturday for those locations. We've also \nadded a tier of counties north of I-70 to the advisory as it \nlooks like they will now reach criteria, though that portion of \nthe advisory will end on Friday.\n\nFrom Sunday into early next week, a broad, slow moving upper trough \nwill push through the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest, finally \nbreaking the grip of the ridge (at least temporarily). An \nassociated cold front will slide into the region, likely late \nSunday or on Monday, bringing some relief to the heat. While \nthere is little doubt the start of next week will be cooler than\nthis week, just how cool it will get remains an open question. \nEnsemble mean high temperatures are right around seasonal \nnormals, but there is notable spread in the guidance, with \nreasonable outcomes ranging from highs in the upper 80 to low \n70s. Along with cooler temperatures, that front is likely bring\nat least a little rain to the region, though at this time there\nis little signal for impactful precipitation.",
